# Content is byte-stable across platforms.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks files out with CRLF while git stores
# LF. THE WHOLE TREE IS NOW PINNED TO LF, rather than a growing list of paths
# that turned out to matter. `text=auto` still lets git detect binary content
# and leave it alone; the exceptions below are belt and braces for the formats
# actually present in this repo, measured with `git ls-files` rather than
# guessed.
#
# ## Why this replaced a list of five numbered cases
#
# The list was reactive. Each entry was added after a CRLF checkout broke
# something, and the fifth was added the day after the fourth. A rule that
# grows by one every time it is tested is not a rule, it is a symptom, and the
# next unpinned path was always going to be found by a gate going red on a
# fresh clone rather than by anyone reading this file.
#
# The five cases are kept below as history, because each names a real failure
# and the reasoning is worth more than the paths were.
#
#   1. `check:content` byte-compares the committed artifact against a fresh
#      generation. A CRLF checkout fails that on every fresh Windows clone,
#      turning a real drift gate into noise.
#   2. The generator embeds markdown source inside the artifact and then into
#      D1, so a CRLF checkout would put CRLF into the stored post body and into
#      what `/blog/:slug.md` serves. The same commit would produce different
#      content depending on which machine ran the generator.
#   3. `build:diagrams` writes its SVG assets with LF and rewrites them in place
#      under `--force`. On a CRLF checkout every asset would come back as a diff
#      the moment anyone forced a rebuild, whether or not the drawing changed.
#   4. `content/llms.txt` is the SOURCE of the `llms.txt` settings row and is
#      compared against that row byte for byte by `check:machine-readable`. The live row is
#      LF, so a CRLF checkout would fail the gate AND sync CRLF into D1,
#      changing what `/llms.txt` serves.
#   5. `CLAUDE.md` is measured by `check:claude-md`, which asserts it fits under
#      the context-window truncation limit and that the hard-rules pointer sits
#      near the top. Both measure the FILE AS THE MACHINE HAS IT. Found
#      2026-08-07, one commit after that gate shipped: the working copy was LF
#      and a fresh clone was CRLF, so the heading needle matched on disk and
#      matched nothing in a clean extraction of the same commit. The gate was
#      green locally and RED on every fresh clone.
#
# The gates were also made line-ending agnostic where they parse text, so this
# file is defence in depth rather than the only thing holding the property up.
#
# ## RESOLVED 2026-08-09: the two files this could not reach
#
# `app/db/index.ts` and `scripts/check-config.mjs` each carried NUL bytes, so
# git's binary auto-detection classified them as binary no matter what was
# written here. Both now use the six-character escape sequence instead of the
# literal byte, so both are text and are covered by the rule above.
#
# The scope of what that cost, stated accurately because an earlier version of
# this comment overstated it: **git diff and ripgrep were blind; the Node-based
# gates never were.** Gates read with `readFileSync`, which is NUL-transparent,
# so `check:invariants` had been scanning `app/db/index.ts` the whole time.
# What was actually lost was REVIEW and SEARCH: diffs rendered as
# `Bin <n> -> <m> bytes`, and a directory-scoped ripgrep skipped both files
# entirely, so the database layer was absent from every `Grep` over `app/`.
#
# `scripts/check-config.mjs` had a second, quieter cost: being binary exempted
# it from LF normalization, so its blob was stored CRLF while every other text
# file was LF. Removing the byte let the rule above finally reach it.
#
# ## EXECUTED 2026-08-11: the working tree was normalized, and autocrlf is off
#
# The pin below governs what git STORES and what a fresh clone GETS. It could
# not reach files already sitting on this disk: anything git had not rewritten
# since `cab1c9e` kept its CRLF bytes locally while its blob was LF.
#
# Measured by the pre-audit sweep: 130 of 274 tracked files differed from their
# committed blob in line endings ONLY, with zero content differences.
# `git status` showed NOTHING, because `core.autocrlf=true` applied the same
# conversion on the way in, so the rot was invisible to the tool most likely to
# be asked about it.
#
# What it cost, measured rather than assumed: `test/visibility-invariants.test.mjs`
# extracts top-level functions by comparing a line to `"}"`. On a CRLF file
# every line ends with a carriage return, so no function ever found its end and
# every body ran to EOF. `app/lib/operator/api.server.ts` is one of the 130, and
# nine of its functions each swallowed the file's two posts queries.
# `app/db/index.ts` hid this for months by happening to be LF.
#
# The repair, once: each of the 130 was rewritten on disk with its own committed
# blob, byte for byte, and `core.autocrlf` was set to FALSE FOR THIS REPOSITORY
# ONLY. Global and system settings are untouched, because autocrlf true is the
# right default for repositories that do not carry the pin below.
#
# Rebuilding `.git/index` was part of it and is worth recording. The index
# stores each file's working-tree SIZE, those sizes were from the CRLF era, and
# neither `update-index --refresh` nor `--really-refresh` would rewrite them:
# `git status` reported 130 modified files while `git diff` was empty and every
# blob SHA matched. The index was rebuilt from HEAD only after proving content
# equality three ways.
#
# THE GATES WERE MADE LINE-ENDING AGNOSTIC ANYWAY. This normalization is the
# belt and the gates are the braces. Do not let a future gate rely on the disk
# having been cleaned.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats present in this repo, by extension, from `git ls-files`.
# Listed explicitly so auto-detection is not the only thing standing between a
# rebuilt font or a photo and a line-ending rewrite.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.ttf   binary
